\section{The two selection axes}{

\code{layers} chooses \strong{which EINs} can be matched (\code{PLAN.md} section 3). Every
EIN has exactly one layer, so a union of layers is a clean union of EINs.
\code{names} and \code{addresses} choose \strong{which strings} those EINs carry, by
variant class. An EIN in an active-BMF layer also has names from its own
filings and from Schedule I; \code{names} decides which of those count.

\code{in_care_of} is not a selectable name class: it names someone other than the
organisation (a person, an agent or a parent). Its organisational content is
in the \code{care_of_network} clusters (\code{\link[=md_build_clusters]{md_build_clusters()}}).
}

\section{Filters that keep statistics exact}{

Corpus statistics for a spec are sums over precomputed cells
(layer x variant class x corroboration band; \code{\link[=md_build_stats]{md_build_stats()}}). A filter
that cuts across cells would break that, so \code{min_corroborators} - which
applies to \code{observed} names only - must be a band edge: 1, 2, 5 or 20.
\code{max_names} and \code{drop_before_year} do cut across cells; a spec using them is
still compiled exactly, but its statistics are computed directly from the
selected rows rather than summed from cells, and the report says so.
